1. ESM strict mode this crash (parser.ts)
The original analyzeFieldNotation accessed this.parentCode without a guard. In CommonJS/sloppy mode, this defaults to globalThis — so it silently worked. But in ESM strict mode, this is undefined, causing:
TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'parentCode')
Fix: this.parentCode → this?.parentCode
2. Two wrong test assertions (parser.test.ts)
The original logic had surprising fallthrough behavior that the test assertions didn't match:
- fn({code: "20"}) when called without a bound context returns true (not false) — because a short code without this hits the final return true fallthrough.
- When parentCode "999" is not in the notation "020$a,856$u", the filtered array is empty and .some() returns false (not true).
3. Non-deterministic tests (helper.test.ts)
Two tests used .find() predicates like f => f.indicator !== undefined to pick "any field with indicators." But some MARC21 records have fields with indicators that aren't in the MARC21 format definition — explainField then returns the field unchanged (no indicators_label), breaking the assertion.
Fix: Narrowed to f => f.code === "245" && f.indicator !== undefined — title field is always defined.
4. Dead code in old marc-node
The original marc-node defined let tabToSpace = (str) => str.replace(/\t/g,"    "); — but never called it anywhere. Removed in the TypeScript port.
5. Type-changing variable pattern (helper.ts)
The original helper.js started formats as a Promise, then reassigned it to the resolved value after loading:
(async () => {
  formats = await formats;
})();
This works in JS but breaks TypeScript's type tracking. The TS version keeps it as Promise<FormatMap> consistently.
6. TypeScript-unfriendly Object.assign pattern (parser.ts)
The Record class initializes all its properties with Object.assign(this, JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(MARC))). TypeScript can't statically prove this, so it flagged 7 properties as uninitialized. Fixed with ! definite assignment assertions.

Here are the two oddities from src/CLI.js:
1. No-op — process.stdout.columns; (line 3)
Reads the property and discards it. Does absolutely nothing. In the TypeScript port, this was rewritten as process.stdout.columns ?? 80 and actually used as a fallback for when the terminal width is undefined (e.g., when piped).
2. out.push[out.length-1] bug — line 15
The developer meant out[out.length-1] (check if the last pushed element equals line to avoid duplicates). But they wrote out.push[out.length-1], which indexes into the push function object as if it were an array — always returning undefined. So undefined != line is always true, and the duplicate-avoidance check is completely dead. The intended logic was:
